# SRG-APP-000439-WSR-000153  -  CAT II
# Web server cookies, such as session cookies, sent to the client using
# SSL/TLS must not be compressed.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations, then kept
# by hand. Edit this file to change how this requirement is assessed here;
# it is read at run time. Regenerating refreshes the benchmark text in place
# and leaves the criteria, the determination and any verification block as
# they are.

id:              SRG-APP-000439-WSR-000153
group:           V-206436
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      documented
cci:             CCI-002418
control_rev5:    SC-8

contribution:    Splunk can refuse TLS layer compression on the web interface, on the
                 splunkd services and on the forwarding output, which is the only
                 compression path that would cover cookie headers, whereas web.conf
                 enable_gzip compresses response bodies and is therefore not decisive for
                 cookies.

governs:         outputs.conf - Where this instance forwards its data, and how that connection is
                 protected.
governs:         server.conf - Core server settings, including the certificates and protocols used to
                 protect management and replication traffic.
governs:         web.conf - Configures the Splunk Web interface.

check:           server.conf [sslConfig] allowSslCompression in 0|false
check:           outputs.conf [tcpout:<group>] useClientSSLCompression in 0|false
# --- vendor verification: generated, do not edit by hand
vendor:          outputs.conf [tcpout:<group>] useClientSSLCompression https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/manage-users-and-security/10.4/install-splunk-enterprise-securely/about-tls-encryption-and-cipher-suites
vendor:          server.conf [sslConfig] allowSslCompression https://help.splunk.com/en/data-management/splunk-enterprise-admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/server.conf
vendor:          web.conf [settings] allowSslCompression https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/manage-users-and-security/10.4/install-splunk-enterprise-securely/about-tls-encryption-and-cipher-suites
# --- end vendor verification

external:        Any TLS terminating reverse proxy or load balancer, whose own compression
                 setting is outside the application
external:        Browser and TLS library behavior, which can offer compression
                 independently of the server
external:        Compression of a cookie on a TLS 1.3 listener, which web.conf [settings]
                 allowSslCompression does not reach: compression at the HTTP layer is
                 governed by enable_gzip, and this instance offers TLS 1.3, so the setting
                 is inert here.

citation:        SRG-APP-000439-WSR-000153 check text; server.conf.spec:1070-1078;
                 web.conf.spec:1176-1183; outputs.conf.spec:764-775; web.conf.spec:346-348
finding:         Splunk permits TLS layer compression on a channel that carries its cookies
                 (server.conf or web.conf allowSslCompression, or outputs.conf
                 useClientSSLCompression, is true), so cookie data is compressed before
                 encryption.
